.TH WAYHUD 1 "2026-09-09" "wayhud" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wayhud \- layer\-shell text overlay for sway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wayhud
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wayhud
displays text on the Wayland overlay layer, then exits after the configured
reveal, hold and vanish. Concurrent invocations create separate overlays.
The overlay does not intercept pointer or keyboard input.
.PP
.I TEXT
is read from the argument, or from standard input when omitted or given as
.BR - .
Messages over 100000 characters are rejected with an error. Stdin reading
stops at EOF or 400001 bytes, whichever comes first; reaching that limit is
an error. Nothing displays before EOF, so a quiet
.B tail \-f
waits indefinitely.
.PP
Arguments expand
.BR \en ,
.B \et
and
.BR \e\e ,
unless
.B \-\-raw
is set. Stdin is used verbatim. Trailing newlines are removed on both paths
to avoid an empty final line in the layout.
.SH OPTIONS
Flags that stand for a config table \-
.BR \-\-outline ,
.BR \-\-glow ,
.BR \-\-position ,
.BR \-\-reveal ,
.BR \-\-vanish ,
.B \-\-sound
\- all take the same
.IR SPEC :
an optional bare value first, then comma\-separated
.IR key = value
pairs. For example:
.B \-\-glow \(aq#b8bb26,radius=12\(aq
sets the glow colour and radius.
.PP
The bare value sets the colour for
.B \-\-glow
and
.BR \-\-outline ,
the kind for
.B \-\-reveal
and
.BR \-\-vanish ,
.BR on / off
for
.BR \-\-sound .
It may also be written by name. Supplying both forms is an error.
.PP
Omitted fields keep the preset's values. Unknown fields, duplicates and
invalid ranges are errors. Field names match TOML, except that outline
.B width
corresponds to
.BR outline_width .
Commas inside brackets, such as in
.BR rgb(184,187,38) ,
are preserved.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fISPEC\fR"
Which outputs to show on:
.B current
(the output sway currently has focus on),
.B all
(every enabled output), or a comma\-separated list of connector names such as
.BR DP\-3,eDP\-1 .
Default:
.BR current .
.IP
.B current
queries sway over its IPC socket. Socket lookup order:
.BR I3SOCK ,
then
.BR SWAYSOCK ,
then asking
.B i3
or
.B sway
for its path. Failure is an error. A named
output that does not exist is reported and skipped; if nothing matches at
all, that is an error.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
How long the fully revealed text stays up. Measured from the
.I end
of the reveal. Defaults to the selected preset's
.BR timeout_ms ,
which is 5 seconds unless the config says otherwise.
.IP
The total reveal, hold and vanish duration is capped at one hour. Overlays
cannot be dismissed early.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-style " \fINAME\fR"
Style preset from the config file. Default:
.BR default .
An unknown name is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-font " \fIDESC\fR"
Pango font description, e.g.\&
.B "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq"
or
.BR "\(dqFiraCode Nerd Font 72\(dq" .
The default is
.BR "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq" ,
the system's fontconfig monospace default. Unavailable named families fall
back silently. Check names with
.BR fc\-match (1).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " \fICOLOUR\fR"
Fill colour of the glyphs. Any colour GTK's CSS parser accepts:
.BR #b8bb26 ,
.BR "rgba(184,187,38,0.8)" ,
.BR green .
.TP
.BR \-\-outline " \fISPEC\fR"
Stroke the glyphs, or
.B none
for flat text. Fields:
.BR color ,
.B width
(logical pixels, 0 to 128).
.IP
Width defaults to font size / 14 unless configured. Supplying width with
.B none
is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-glow " \fISPEC\fR"
Paint a soft halo behind the glyphs, or
.B none
for no halo. Fields:
.BR color ,
.B radius
(logical pixels, 0 to 128),
.B alpha
(0 to 1).
.IP
The halo is drawn behind the outline. Increasing its radius adds padding
and reduces the available wrapping width.
.TP
.BR \-\-scanlines " \fISPEC\fR"
Cut dimmed horizontal bands through the message, the way a raster scan does,
or
.B none
for a flat one. The bare value is
.BR period .
Fields:
.B period
(device pixels between band centres, 2 to 128),
.B strength
(how far a band dims what is under it, 0 to 1),
.B duty
(fraction of each period the band covers, 0 to below 1).
.IP
The bands are measured in
.I device
pixels, so the raster belongs to the screen and does not scale with the font:
one period is one period on a HiDPI output too. They are applied over the
finished message, so they cut the glow along with the glyphs, and they do not
widen the padding.
.IP
.B strength
of 0 switches off a raster inherited from a preset.
.TP
.BR \-\-position " \fISPEC\fR"
Where the text block sits. The bare value is a placement word:
.BR center ,
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
or a hyphenated pair such as
.B top\-left
or
.BR bottom\-right ,
in either order. A bare value sets both axes:
.B bottom
means bottom\-centre. Fields:
.BR halign ,
.BR valign ,
which set one axis each and leave the other alone. A centred axis ignores the
margin.
.TP
.BR \-\-margin " \fIPX\fR"
Gap from the anchored edge to the surface, in logical pixels. The surface
includes padding for the outline, glow and caret. Centred axes ignore it.
.TP
.BR \-\-width " \fIPX\fR"
Width of the text block in logical pixels, excluding padding. Unset, lines
wrap to the monitor and the surface is sized from the measured text. Set, it
is both the wrapping budget and the width of the block, so the surface keeps
that width whatever the message. Clamped to the monitor.
.TP
.BR \-\-lines " \fIN\fR"
Height of the text block in lines. Unset, the surface is sized from the
message. Set, the room is reserved up front, so a message growing inside the
block does not resize the surface. Since the surface is transparent, reserved
room a message has not reached is not visible.
.TP
.BR \-\-line\-align " \fIALIGN\fR"
Alignment of the lines within the text block:
.BR left ,
.B center
or
.BR right .
Distinct from
.BR \-\-position ,
which places the block itself.
.TP
.BR \-\-reveal " \fISPEC\fR"
How the text appears. The bare value is the kind:
.B instant
or
.BR typewriter .
Fields, all belonging to the typewriter:
.B cps
(characters per second, positive),
.B cursor
(true/false),
.B jitter
(0 to 1),
.B scroll
(true/false).
.IP
.B jitter
varies each keystroke interval by up to the specified fraction in either
direction. Audio uses the same timings.
.IP
.B scroll
keeps the current line at the bottom and moves earlier lines up. See
.B TERMINAL MODE
below.
.IP
Typewriter fields require a typewriter preset or an explicit
.BR "\-\-reveal \(aqtypewriter,cps=50\(aq" .
.TP
.BR \-\-vanish " \fISPEC\fR"
How the text goes away. The bare value is the kind:
.BR instant ,
.BR fade ,
.BR collapse ,
.BR wash ,
.BR untype ,
.BR dissolve .
Fields:
.B ms
(duration; not accepted on
.BR instant ),
.B dir
.RB ( up / down ,
only on
.BR wash ).
.RS
.TP
.B instant
Gone on the frame the hold expires.
.TP
.B fade
Alpha to zero.
.TP
.B collapse
CRT power\-off: squashes to a bright line, blooms slightly wider, washes to
white, blinks out.
.TP
.B wash
A soft edge sweeps through the text and erases it as it passes.
.TP
.B untype
Erase characters in reverse order with sound and a caret, including after
an instant reveal.
.TP
.B dissolve
The text falls apart into blocks, in a fixed pseudo\-random order.
.RE
.IP
Omitting
.B ms
preserves the preset's duration, or uses 420 ms when switching from instant.
.B \-\-vanish ms=800
changes only the duration.
.TP
.BR \-\-sound " \fISPEC\fR"
The typewriter blip. The bare value is
.B on
or
.BR off .
Fields:
.B enabled
(true/false),
.B freq
(100 to 8000 Hz),
.B decay_ms
(10 to 3000),
.B gain
(0 to 1),
.B every
(blip once every N revealed characters, at least 1).
.IP
Switching sound on or off preserves its other parameters.
.TP
.B \-\-listen
Stay up and show what arrives on the socket, instead of showing one message
and exiting. Takes no
.IR TEXT .
See
.B LISTENER
below.
.TP
.B \-\-send
Send
.I TEXT
to a running listener instead of showing it here, and exit. The style belongs
to the listener, so every other flag is ignored.
.TP
.BR \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Socket to listen on or send to. Default:
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wayhud.sock .
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Take
.I TEXT
literally: do not expand
.BR \en ,
.B \et
or
.BR \e\e .
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIPATH\fR"
Config file to read. Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml ,
or
.I ~/.config/wayhud/config.toml
when that variable is unset, empty or relative.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH TERMINAL MODE
The default typewriter fills the block from the top.
.B \-\-reveal scroll=true
keeps the current line at the bottom and scrolls earlier lines up.
.B \-\-reveal scroll=false
restores the default mode.
.PP
Both modes use a fixed surface sized for the complete message and work with
any position. Single-line messages render identically. During
.BR untype ,
the scroll reverses as lines are erased.
.SH LISTENER
.B wayhud \-\-listen
binds a datagram socket and shows what arrives on it, one message per
datagram. It is the same overlay drawn the same way; what differs is where the
message comes from and how long the process stays.
.PP
A datagram rather than a stream or a FIFO, so that one send is one message:
a notification body carrying newlines needs no framing and no escaping. It
also fails the right way round. Opening a FIFO for writing blocks until a
reader arrives, so a hook run from a notification daemon would leave a process
hung for every notification while nothing was listening; sending to a socket
nobody is bound to returns an error at once.
.PP
Anything that can write a datagram can be a client:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ft B
printf '%s' \(dqDISK 0 OK\(dq | socat \- UNIX\-SENDTO:\(dq$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wayhud.sock\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B wayhud \-\-send
does the same without the extra program, and reads
.I TEXT
from an argument or standard input exactly as the one-shot mode does. Pick a
third-party tool with care:
.B nc \-uU
delivers and then waits for a reply that never comes, so it hangs unless
something kills it.
.SS What arrives, and when it goes
A message joins the one on screen rather than replacing it. While the block is
being revealed or held, an arrival is appended as a line, only that line is
typed, and the hold starts again from it. A vanish is a commit point: what
arrives during one waits for it to finish and then starts a block of its own,
so a burst cannot hold the overlay up forever.
.PP
The block is bounded. It keeps the last
.B lines
of text, or ten lines when no reservation is set, dropping from the top.
.PP
Reserve the block with
.B \-\-lines
and
.BR \-\-width .
Both are what stop the surface resizing as the message grows, and a surface
that resizes is one the compositor places again on every notification.
.B \-\-reveal \(aqscroll=true\(aq
then reads as a terminal: the newest line stays on the bottom of the block and
the earlier ones rise.
.PP
The style is the listener's. A client sends text and nothing else, so a
notification hook cannot decide what the overlay looks like, and the block can
be sized before the first message arrives.
.PP
The socket is created with owner-only permission under
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
which is itself private. A path left behind by a listener that did not exit
cleanly is reused; one that something still answers on is refused.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B wayhud
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml
(falling back to
.IR ~/.config/wayhud/config.toml ).
A missing file uses built-in defaults. Malformed files and unknown keys
are errors.
.PP
The file is a flat map of named presets, selected with
.BR \-\-style .
.B [style.default]
is the base for every other preset: a key a preset does not set is taken from
there, and only then from the built\-in default. Command\-line flags override
the selected preset for that invocation.
.PP
Every key, its type and its default are documented in
.BR wayhud (5).
.SH SOUND
Blip parameters match
.BR blyamk (1)
and can be copied from
.B blyamk \-v
into a
.B [style.X.sound]
table.
.PP
Tracks are synthesised before display and played through PulseAudio using
the animation's character timings. The untype track starts after the hold.
Audio failures are reported on standard error; the message still displays.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
A message on the focused output, default style:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \(dqSYSTEM ONLINE\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
Ten seconds, every screen, pinned to the top:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \-o all \-t 10 \-\-position top \(dqBUILD FAILED\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
Two lines from a keybinding in the sway config:
.nf
.ft B
bindsym $mod+Shift+h exec wayhud \(dqLOCKED\enBACK IN 5\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
Piped in, no animation, no sound:
.nf
.ft B
echo \(dq$(uptime)\(dq | wayhud \-\-reveal instant \-\-sound off
.ft R
.fi
.TP
Self\-destructing note:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \-s spy \-\-vanish \(aquntype,ms=900\(aq \e
       \(dqTHIS MESSAGE WILL SELF DESTRUCT\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
A boot log crawling up the bottom corner:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \-\-reveal \(aqscroll=true\(aq \-\-position bottom\-left \e
       \(dqCHECKING DISKS\enMOUNTING /\enOK\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
A long line, wrapped into a fixed block:
.nf
.ft B
journalctl \-n 1 \-u nginx | wayhud \-\-width 900 \-\-reveal instant
.ft R
.fi
.TP
A green tube in the corner:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \-\-scanlines \(aq4,strength=0.5\(aq \-\-glow \(aq#33ff33\(aq \e
       \-\-color \(aq#33ff33\(aq \-\-position bottom\-left \(dqDISK 0 OK\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.TP
A quieter, slower blip:
.nf
.ft B
wayhud \-\-sound \(aqfreq=1200,gain=0.1,every=2\(aq \(dqSTANDING BY\(dq
.ft R
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B I3SOCK
Path to the compositor's IPC socket, checked first. Set by
.BR i3 ;
sway honours it too.
.TP
.B SWAYSOCK
Path to sway's IPC socket, checked when
.B I3SOCK
is unset. One of the two, or a working
.BR "sway \-\-get\-socketpath" ,
is required for
.BR "\-\-output current" .
.TP
.B GSK_RENDERER
Selects GTK's renderer. If Vulkan produces
.B vkQueuePresentKHR
/
.B VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR
warnings, try
.B opengl
or
.BR cairo .
wayhud leaves this setting to GTK.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the config file, used only when it holds an
.I absolute
path. Unset, empty or relative, it falls back to
.IR $HOME/.config .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message was shown and its lifetime ran out.
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, unreadable or invalid config, no matching output, or no
display.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wayhud (5),
.BR sway (1),
.BR swaymsg (1)
